# domain.md.example

This file shows what `R0: init` produces. It's a reference, not a
live config — the real one is written to `domain.md` at the project
root after the operator runs the init interview.

The schema below is annotated. Three short worked examples follow,
showing how the same fields adapt across very different topics.

---

## Schema

```yaml
# What the operator is researching, in their own words.
topic: "One or two sentences. Field of inquiry where reasonable
        people would disagree about sub-fields."

# 2–5 kinds of things this topic studies. Used as wiki page-type
# values and tag families.
concept_categories:
  - "category-name"      # lowercase, kebab-case
  - "category-name"

# 3–8 sub-fields, specialties, or schools that cut across the topic.
# These become the domain tags R2 uses to find bridges.
subfields:
  - "subfield-name"
  - "subfield-name"

# 2–5 primary-source databases / archives / repositories the agent
# should look in first when searching for evidence.
primary_sources:
  - name: "Source name"
    url_pattern: "https://…"
    identifier_scheme: "doi"     # doi | pmid | arxiv | isbn | rfc |
                                 # case-docket | url-hash | …

# Whether R2 (cross-domain probe) is enabled. Set true when the topic
# pays off on connections between sub-fields; false for deep single-
# discipline dives.
cross_domain: true

# Optional. Seed question to kick off the first R1 or R5 after init.
seed_question: "The first thing the operator wants to look at."

# Optional. Identifier-scheme preference order for source slugs,
# overriding the default DOI > PMID > arXiv > ISBN > url-hash.
identifier_priority:
  - doi
  - pmid
  - arxiv
  - isbn
  - url-hash

# Optional. Source-type taxonomy specific to this topic. Helps R3
# fill in meta.yaml → source_type consistently.
source_types:
  - "RCT"
  - "cohort study"
  - "review"
  - "book"
  - "court opinion"
  # …whatever the operator's field uses
```

---

## Example 1 — Cross-domain medical research

```yaml
topic: "Cross-domain effects of supplements, drugs, behaviors, and
        diet on organ systems and conditions. Particular interest in
        findings that bridge specialties (e.g., gut↔brain)."

concept_categories:
  - intervention       # supplements, drugs, foods, behaviors
  - organ-system       # gut, brain, heart, skin, immune, …
  - condition          # IBS, eczema, depression, migraine, …

subfields:
  - gastroenterology
  - psychiatry
  - dermatology
  - endocrinology
  - immunology
  - sleep-medicine
  - sports-medicine

primary_sources:
  - name: PubMed
    url_pattern: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=…"
    identifier_scheme: pmid
  - name: Cochrane
    url_pattern: "https://www.cochranelibrary.com/search?q=…"
    identifier_scheme: doi
  - name: bioRxiv / medRxiv
    url_pattern: "https://www.biorxiv.org/search/…"
    identifier_scheme: doi

cross_domain: true

seed_question: "What's the strongest evidence that magnesium glycinate
                affects mood, and is the mechanism understood?"

source_types:
  - RCT
  - cohort
  - case-control
  - animal
  - in-vitro
  - meta-analysis
  - review
```

## Example 2 — ML research

```yaml
topic: "Attention mechanisms and their alternatives in large language
        models. How architecture choices interact with training data
        and downstream benchmark performance."

concept_categories:
  - technique          # attention variants, state-space models, MoE, …
  - model              # GPT-4, Claude, Llama, Mamba, …
  - benchmark          # MMLU, HumanEval, GSM8K, …
  - phenomenon         # in-context learning, scaling laws, grokking, …

subfields:
  - architecture
  - training-dynamics
  - interpretability
  - evaluation
  - alignment

primary_sources:
  - name: arXiv
    url_pattern: "https://arxiv.org/abs/…"
    identifier_scheme: arxiv
  - name: OpenReview
    url_pattern: "https://openreview.net/forum?id=…"
    identifier_scheme: url-hash
  - name: Papers with Code
    url_pattern: "https://paperswithcode.com/search?q=…"
    identifier_scheme: arxiv

cross_domain: true   # architecture papers rarely cite eval papers and vice versa

seed_question: "Where do attention-based models reliably outperform
                state-space models, and what's the explanation?"

source_types:
  - empirical study
  - theoretical paper
  - position paper
  - benchmark paper
  - blog post
```

## Example 3 — Single-discipline deep dive (history)

```yaml
topic: "The diplomatic history of the Treaty of Westphalia (1648) —
        how it was negotiated, what it actually changed, and how
        later historians have reinterpreted it."

concept_categories:
  - event              # individual negotiations, signings, congresses
  - actor              # delegates, monarchs, ministers
  - doctrine           # sovereignty, cuius regio, jus publicum
  - place              # Münster, Osnabrück, Imperial territories

subfields:
  - early-modern-european-history
  - international-relations
  - religious-history
  - legal-history

primary_sources:
  - name: Acta Pacis Westphalicae (digital edition)
    url_pattern: "http://www.pax-westphalica.de/…"
    identifier_scheme: url-hash
  - name: JSTOR
    url_pattern: "https://www.jstor.org/stable/…"
    identifier_scheme: doi
  - name: Internet Archive (primary docs)
    url_pattern: "https://archive.org/details/…"
    identifier_scheme: url-hash

cross_domain: false   # a focused historical study, not an interdisciplinary probe

seed_question: "How much of 'Westphalian sovereignty' was actually
                established in 1648, and how much is a 20th-century
                back-projection?"

source_types:
  - primary document
  - monograph
  - peer-reviewed article
  - edited volume
```

---

## Notes on use

- `domain.md` is not immutable — R6 can suggest edits if patterns in
  the corpus reveal that a sub-field is missing or a category is
  miscalibrated. The operator confirms before edits land.
- If the topic genuinely pivots (the operator changes what they're
  researching), re-run R0 rather than patching `domain.md` piecemeal.
- The agent should read `domain.md` at the start of every session and
  carry it as context. It's the difference between "research assistant
  for some topic" and "research assistant for **this** topic."
